question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,1):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-1)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,1]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-1)^{+}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49889/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83d52a4-6f69-5607-8753-e96c13bd7209"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49889"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82842/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "247087b3-7a64-5cb7-9d22-ccfb3696c981" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82842/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "37be8d72-636a-588b-9a28-dd66200a86ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82842/selection/a2f6a61b26c9b21274b850392174ed23088562f19fd3dd8faa53fa3d298a85ef" :answer/id #uuid "7af2cabc-9776-5c72-bfe0-e8e1ebb63898" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82842/selection/634196fc9bb48033d39ff9813af63434068332cb8c836d13c5213b0cd4323e90" :answer/id #uuid "8e4e806c-26c8-51d6-8798-f36eb0f6abe5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82842/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" :answer/id #uuid "3014329a-929d-5e94-b59a-33a8f4d7d3fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82842/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "089f1ec9-ba3d-55b3-8ad0-925a7d01e899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82842/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" :answer-field/choices ["q-82842/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-82842/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-82842/selection/a2f6a61b26c9b21274b850392174ed23088562f19fd3dd8faa53fa3d298a85ef" "q-82842/selection/634196fc9bb48033d39ff9813af63434068332cb8c836d13c5213b0cd4323e90" "q-82842/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82842" :question/id #uuid "f8b525b2-3937-540a-90ac-044fa724bc45" :question/math-academy-id "q-82842" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral  ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{u}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2}{a}| \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82842/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82842"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49894/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "e685dcce-5e98-5680-a324-25efdf276b3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49894/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "9920d468-65c0-557b-a25c-5a879c915609" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49894/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" :answer/id #uuid "fd9c2f8f-8f29-5566-8ddf-6aaa86b24d4b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49894/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "c281505a-9e30-59a4-a596-e0a321c18948" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49894/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "9dbd8a42-7d17-539f-b8a6-d76cc4a649af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49894/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "abd31044-ca4b-5c77-8e41-f2c01b39f93e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49894/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-49894/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-49894/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-49894/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" "q-49894/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-49894/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49894" :question/id #uuid "b8978839-8740-53c7-a939-fdd329cbc12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49894" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}4{x}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5{x}^{4/5}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{1}^{4}}-5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =5(1)-5(0) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49894/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49894"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115353/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" :answer/id #uuid "a2333b34-e8b2-5506-ac82-caedc47e5776" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115353/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" :answer/id #uuid "5df580a9-0eaa-5ae1-82df-bdaf3844274d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115353/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "8ffd87f0-df5a-5032-bbb5-b4b9f2b5e83a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115353/selection/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e" :answer/id #uuid "55354bac-7dbf-5020-91fd-d329bffbc266" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115353/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "022ddb0f-863d-56b7-b9b2-ae84f2558c65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115353/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e2cd14d-b1e9-5fa5-9bc6-9332fff67489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115353/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" :answer-field/choices ["q-115353/selection/8bb646af439794acfbd313a6a7eb86a2818f1f792118f7583677724bca2d9903" "q-115353/selection/7ea009d38067b85c260cd71ce15cce585e1f931f7adf440bc470127b22bd36c2" "q-115353/selection/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-115353/selection/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e" "q-115353/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115353" :question/id #uuid "06043800-b433-5131-a08a-adba5684db0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115353" :question/problem "${\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3}{(3x-2)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{(3x-2)^{5}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}\\,{u}^{-4})|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{256}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-115353/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115353"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-6039"] :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=4-t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}} & ={\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{3}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{2{u}^{1/2}|}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{2\\sqrt{u}|}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[2\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{a}] \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}-0 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-351487/field-1/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "9bd7fe64-d0d0-5327-9057-c42c762ba23c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351487/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "071028ed-551a-5c50-a5d1-3a22d871c65c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351487/field-1/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer-field/choices ["q-351487/field-1/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351487" :question/id #uuid "1dc9e4a0-dba4-54bb-8093-9583c6a7e4ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-351487" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)={u}^{-1/5}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{5}{4}{u}^{4/5}|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/5}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {a}^{4/5}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-351487/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf1d89ed-0d2a-5759-9e6e-354957f7dabc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351487"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7297"] :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x-2}\\qquad$  II. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{0} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x+2}\\qquad$  III. $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{3} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x^2-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite. So, it is not an improper integral of the second kind.\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow (-2)^+.\n$$\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits, and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in[2,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9959"] :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,{\\color{blue}{1}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) =   \\dfrac {1}{x^2-1} \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{1}} ^-\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0, b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to{\\color{blue}1}^-\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} = \\lim\\limits_{b \\to {\\color{blue}{1}}^-} \\left( \\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{b} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}}   \\right)\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-517"] :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & =-(-\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
